.Dd November 23, 2026
.Dt ZFS-FILEBLOCKSIZE 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-fileblocksize
.Nd choose the block size of files and directories in a ZFS filesystem
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Oo Fl d Ns | Ns Fl r Ns Oc
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Fl C
.Op Fl r
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Fl c
.Oo Fl 0 Ns Oc
.Oo Fl d Ns | Ns Fl r Ns Oc
.Op Fl b Ar size
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Fl b Ar size
.Op Fl r
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A file's block size normally grows with the file, up to the dataset's
.Sy recordsize .
A file with a fixed internal page size, such as a database, can instead be
given a block size that matches its pages, without changing
.Sy recordsize
for the rest of the dataset.
The block size of an empty regular file can be chosen, and the file keeps it
as it grows.
A directory can carry a block size that new regular files created in it get,
and that new subdirectories inherit, so that files an application creates
itself, such as a database's journal, get it too.
The size must be a power of 2 from 512 bytes to 128 KiB, no larger than
.Sy recordsize ,
and larger than
.Sy special_small_blocks ,
so that a file's blocks only go to the special allocation class if blocks of
.Sy recordsize
would.
A directory stops giving its size to new files while the size breaks either
limit; files already set keep their size.
The setting can't be changed on an immutable file or directory.
.Pp
Setting a file's block size waits for the pool to sync the change, so setting
many files with
.Fl r
is slow.
Where possible, set the directory before its files are created: new files
then get the size at no extra cost.
Block cloning, such as
.Nm cp Fl -reflink ,
into a file whose block size differs from the source's fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
so a clone into a directory with a setting may instead copy the data or fail.
.Pp
These are the extent size settings of the
.Dv FS_IOC_FSGETXATTR
and
.Dv FS_IOC_FSSETXATTR
ioctls:
.Dv FS_XFLAG_EXTSIZE
on a regular file and
.Dv FS_XFLAG_EXTSZINHERIT
on a directory, with the size in
.Fa fsx_extsize .
Applications may also set them directly.
Only the owner of a file or directory, or a privileged user, may change them.
The
.Sy fileblocksize
property, described in
.Xr zfsprops 7 ,
controls whether new settings are allowed.
.Bl -tag -width ""
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Oo Fl d Ns | Ns Fl r Ns Oc
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.Xc
List the block size setting of files and directories: the size, or 0 for
none, then
.Sy I
for a directory that gives its size to new files, and the name.
.Bl -tag -width "-d"
.It Fl d
Show the directory's own setting, not its children's.
.It Fl r
List subdirectories recursively.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Fl C
.Op Fl r
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.Xc
Clear the setting on files and directories.
A file that already has data keeps its block size; one that is still empty
grows toward
.Sy recordsize
again.
.Bl -tag -width "-r"
.It Fl r
Clear subdirectories and their files recursively.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Fl c
.Oo Fl 0 Ns Oc
.Oo Fl d Ns | Ns Fl r Ns Oc
.Op Fl b Ar size
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.Xc
Check the setting on files and directories: report entries whose setting
differs from the target directory's, or from
.Ar size .
Files that had data before the directory's setting was made are reported
this way.
.Bl -tag -width "-b size"
.It Fl 0
Delimit filenames with a NUL byte instead of newline, don't output diagnoses.
.It Fl b Ar size
Compare to
.Ar size
instead of the target directory's setting.
.It Fl d
Check the directory's own setting, not its children's.
.It Fl r
Check subdirectories recursively.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm fileblocksize
.Fl b Ar size
.Op Fl r
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns …
.Xc
Set the block size of empty regular files, and the size directories give to
new files and subdirectories.
Naming a file that already has data is an error.
.Bl -tag -width "-r"
.It Fl r
Set on subdirectories and empty files recursively.
Files that already have data are left as they are.
.El
.El
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1 : No A directory for SQLite databases with 16 KiB pages
.Bd -literal -compact -offset Ds
.No # Nm zfs Cm fileblocksize Fl b Ar 16K Pa /tank/db
$ sqlite3 /tank/db/app.db \e
    'PRAGMA page_size=16384; PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL; CREATE TABLE t(x);'
.No $ Nm zfs Cm fileblocksize Pa /tank/db
  16384 I /tank/db
  16384 - /tank/db/app.db
.Ed
.Pp
The database's
.Pa -wal
and
.Pa -journal
files get the same block size while they exist.
.
